\section{Ranking Repairs Within a Single Statement}

An editor can compare small repair candidates without mutating the
document or repeatedly synchronizing at the end of the file. Take a
parser checkpoint immediately before the error and a host-delimited
window of real tokens that the current statement owns. The procedure
below considers inserting one expected punctuation token or skipping
the first real token in the window. Assume the parser can replay a
checkpoint without committing actions and require a candidate to
parse to the known statement boundary. The result is a provisional
tree with one marked repair, or an error region containing the entire
window. It is not an automatic edit to the user's text.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input checkpoint $s$, statement suffix $W$, expected set $E$
  \Output recovered node or an error region
  \State $best\gets\mathsf{none}$
  \State $C\gets\Call{Insertions}{E,W}$
  \If{$W$ begins with a nonboundary token}
    \State add $\Call{SkipFirst}{W}$ to $C$
    \Comment{Retain skipped token in the tree}
  \EndIf
  \ForAll{candidate $c$ in $C$}
    \State $r\gets\Call{Replay}{s,c}$
    \Comment{No effects on the live parser}
    \If{$r$ succeeds at the boundary}
      \State $best\gets\Call{Prefer}{best,r}$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $best$ or $\Call{ErrorRegion}{W}$
\end{sudocode}

Let \texttt{emit 1 2;} be a malformed command in a language that
expects either a comma-separated argument list or a single argument.
At the second integer the expected punctuation is comma or semicolon.
The suffix window contains \texttt{2 ;}, including the original
terminator. Inserting a comma produces a two-argument node with a
zero-width separator at the start of \texttt{2}. Inserting a
semicolon would leave \texttt{2 ;} unconsumed, so that candidate
fails the boundary test. Skipping \texttt{2} produces a one-argument
node with an unexpected-token child. Prefer the comma repair by
ranking successful candidates first by real tokens retained in
ordinary syntax, then by a stable punctuation order. No candidate
consumes any part of the following command.

The preservation invariant is that every source token belongs to
the normal tree or to an explicit error child. Boundary tokens
cannot disappear by deletion, and synthetic tokens have no source
width. The cost is at most $|E|+1$ replay attempts, each bounded
by the window and the parser's per-attempt work limit. On a timeout,
do not publish a guessed repair as though it had reached the
boundary. Nested constructs need an appropriate host checkpoint:
a semicolon inside a string cannot be the window terminator.
